<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eritrean People's Liberation Front</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1970s–<a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as EPLF</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eritrean People's Liberation Front was the armed movement that won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>'s independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Formed in the early 1970s by groups that broke away from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-liberation-front">Eritrean Liberation Front</a>, it built a disciplined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> army based in the mountains around Nakfa. It took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara</a> in May 1991, and after the 1993 referendum its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaias-afwerki">Isaias Afwerki</a> became president; in 1994 it became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-front-for-democracy-and-justice">People's Front for Democracy and Justice</a>, the sole legal party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eritrean-peoples-liberation-front.jpg" alt="Eritrean People's Liberation Front" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Permjak · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_EPLF.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1967 thirty-three members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-liberation-front">Eritrean Liberation Front</a> (ELF), among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaias-afwerki">Isaias Afwerki</a> and Romodan Mohammed Nur, trained for six months in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. On their return they strengthened the front but also sharpened its feuds. Many young dissidents, disillusioned with feuding leaders, left the ELF, and the Eritrean People's Liberation Front emerged from them in August 1973. Led by Isaias and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a>, it found refuge in the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> and gave its members political as well as military training, with the same education for all.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Internal dissent was put down by Isaias, and eleven members were executed in August 1974. The front's First Congress in January 1977 elected Romodan Mohammed Nur secretary-general and called for women's rights, literacy in every language and borders based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s colonial treaties. Between 1975 and 1977 the ELF and EPLF overran most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, but a counteroffensive in 1978, backed by Soviet support that eventually exceeded $11 billion, forced a strategic withdrawal. In a second civil war from August 1980 the EPLF, helped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigray-peoples-liberation-front">Tigray People's Liberation Front</a>, defeated the ELF.</p><p>Between February and June 1982 more than 80,000 Ethiopian troops failed to crush the front in the Red Star Campaign. Its army grew from about four hundred men into a force of divisions and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a>, nearly a third of it women, though few women held command. It also used child soldiers between 1979 and 1983, a policy dropped after criticism. At the Unity Congress of 1987 Isaias became secretary-general and the movement abandoned most of its Marxist–Leninist ideology for a National Democratic Program.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In March 1988 the EPLF destroyed the strongest Ethiopian garrison in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-afabet">Battle of Afabet</a>, which has been likened to Điện Biên Phủ. In 1990 it took the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a>; Ethiopian bombing that followed destroyed many historic Islamic buildings there. Allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigray-peoples-liberation-front">TPLF</a>, it helped bring down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a> in May 1991. At its Third Congress, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara</a> in 1994, the front, then 95,000 members strong, became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-front-for-democracy-and-justice">People's Front for Democracy and Justice</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eritrean People's Liberation Front” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By late 1976 insurgencies existed in all fourteen administrative regions: the <b>Eritrean People's Liberation Front</b> fought for Eritrean independence, rebels in Tigray included the nascent Tigray People's Liberation Front, and the Western Somali Liberation Front had by June 1977 driven the army out of much of the Ogaden…</blockquote><a href="/empire/derg">Derg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its army, among Africa's largest, was badly overstretched by wars with the <b>Eritrean People's Liberation Front</b> and the Tigrayan-led EPRDF, and suffered a heavy defeat at Afabet in 1988.</blockquote><a href="/state/peoples-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">People's Democratic Republic of Ethiopia</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Eritrean People's Liberation Front</b> took Asmara in May 1991, ending the war that began in 1961 as Ethiopia dismantled the Eritrea federation.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-eritrea">State of Eritrea</a></li></ul></section>
